    "q-162685": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162685",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-4}\\,i+t\\,j+\\sqrt{t-2}\\,k.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component $\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}-4}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty -2)\\cup (-2,2)\\cup (2,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{t-2}$ is $t\\in [2,\\infty ).$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14242901/assets/18bd3e410f2f3e415e8164ae6e2f9e03a2f7281ce15e6e05cb570ecfdac10964.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (2,\\infty ).$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
