    "q-162680": {
      "dom_id": "step-q162680",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "Find the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)=({t}^{2}+t)\\,i+\\frac{1}{2+2t}\\,j+\\sqrt{2-t}\\,k.$",
      "worked_solution": "To find the domain of $f$, we need the intersection of the domains of the individual components.\n\n- The domain of the 1st component ${t}^{2}+t$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 2nd component $\\frac{1}{2+2t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,\\infty ).$\n\n- The domain of the 3rd component $\\sqrt{2-t}$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,2].$\n\nThe intersection is illustrated in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14242901/assets/61138db6d0965f64d225d694f90e237955db1e5adfb67cfbf6adfae82520f3aa.png)\n\nTherefore, the domain of the vector-valued function $f(t)$ is $t\\in (-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (-1,2].$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
